   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 929 of the laws of 1986 amending the tax law and other laws relating to the metropolitan transportation authority, in relation to extending certain provisions thereof applicable to the resolution of labor disputes",
    "basePrintNo": "S5257",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5257-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §45, Chap 929 of 1986",
    "lawSection": "Labor",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5257\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend chapter 929 of the laws of 1986\namending the tax law and other laws relating to the metropolitan\ntransportation authority, in relation to extending certain provisions\nthereof applicable to the resolution of labor disputes\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThis bill will extend for two years, from July 1, 2017 to July 1,\n2019, certain provisions of Tax Law and other laws scheduled to\nexpire, which authorize the oversight of binding arbitration by the\nPublic Employment Relations Board (PERB) between the Metropolitan\nTransportation Authority (MTA) and its labor representatives in order\nto promote settlements and avoid disruptions in transit service.\n\nEffective immediately, this bill extends the authorization for the\nPERB to engage the following parties in binding interest arbitration\nif labor negotiations fail: The Metropolitan Transportation Authority,\nincluding its subsidiary, the NYC Transit Authority; The Triborough\nBridge and Tunnel Authority; and the public employee organizations\ncertified or recognized to represent the majority of employees from\nthe above-mentioned public authorities.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 would amend section 45 of Chapter 929 of the Laws of 1986 to\nextend the July 1, 2017 sunset provision to July 1, 2019.\n\nSection 2 would make the bill effective immediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nCompulsory arbitration has promoted the resolution of labor management\ndisputes in furtherance of uninterrupted service on the MTA transit\nsystem and its bridges and tunnels. Evaluations of the effects of this\nlaw have historically been positive. This will extend this provision\nfor an additional two years.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThe sunset provision in question has been extended every two years;\nspecifically, by Chapter 28 of the Laws of 2015, Chapter 72 of the\nLaws of 2013, Chapter 105 of the Laws of 2011, Chapter 93 of the Laws\nof 2009, Chapter 168 of the Laws of 2007, Chapter 46 of the Laws of\n2005, Chapter 111 of the Laws of 2003, Chapter 81 of the Laws of 2001\nand Chapter 150 of the Laws of 1999.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThis bill would have no direct impact on the state's budget.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe bill would take effect immediately.\n",
